      "question_id": "v2q22",
      "expected_answerable": true,
      "abstained": false,
      "top_score": 0.03278688524590164,
      "answer": "The framework covering principles, primitives, pooling, learning, and clustering for temporal graphs can be found in [2608.03696v1]. This work focuses on learning and clustering on temporal graphs, with particular emphasis on the task of clustering, and frames the tension through three linked perspectives: principles, primitives, and pooling. \n\nThe principles perspective connects graph learning and community detection through shared spectral foundations and detectability thresholds in stochastic block model regimes. The primitives perspective makes spectral clustering and multislice modularity optimization tractable through GPU-accelerated temporal backends. The pooling perspective views principled community detection as a form of pooling in machine learning on graphs.\n\nWhile other papers, such as [2608.03695v1] and [2608.02128v1], also discuss graph clustering and community detection, [2608.03696v1] is the most relevant to the question as it explicitly covers the principles, primitives, pooling, learning, and clustering for temporal graphs.",
